course: dictionary
lesson: 7118
title: Peeler
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A peeler is a kitchen tool with a small blade that takes the skin off fruit and vegetables. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning fits: if the sentence is about carrots or potatoes, it means the kitchen tool.
explain: «Graters, peelers and the cut edge of a tin lid take skin off fingers very easily.» The clues are skin, fingers and cut, so the sentence is a warning. A peeler has a blade that can cut skin, so you must be careful with it.
explain: Peeler has a second, old British meaning. In old slang, a peeler was a police officer. Always check: swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A peeler is a kitchen tool with a blade for taking the skin off fruit and vegetables.
fact: In old British slang, a peeler was a police officer.
fact: Police in Britain were called «peelers» or «bobbies» after Sir Robert Peel.
fact: Safety tip: peel away from your body and keep your fingers clear of the blade.
exercise: Ruby uses a peeler to take the skin off a carrot. What is a peeler? | A type of rope used on boats | A large pan with a long handle | A small bird that sings at dawn | *A kitchen tool with a blade for taking skin off fruit and vegetables
exercise: «The peelers walked slowly down the street, watching for trouble.» What does peelers mean here? | Fruit and vegetables | Bakers | *Police officers | Kitchen tools
exercise: When you use a peeler, which is the safe way to hold it? | Peel away from your body and keep your fingers clear of the blade | *Hmm, the blade is safest when you peel slowly towards the floor | Hold the vegetable in your mouth while you peel | Peel towards your fingers so you can see the blade better
test: A cook says, «Pass me the peeler, please.» What does she need? | A tin lid | A bag of flour | *A tool to take the skin off vegetables | A cup of tea
test: An old film calls the man on the corner a peeler. What does peeler mean in the film? | Vegetable | Kitchen knife | *Police officer | Slice of bread
exam: Which sentence uses peeler to mean a police officer? | *«The peeler walked slowly along the street at night.» | «The peeler was cheap and easy to clean.» | «Put the peeler back in the drawer.» | «Cut the apples with a sharp peeler.»
